answer_text:
During the **2017–2021 Qatar diplomatic crisis**, the UAE said its civilian nuclear program—especially the Barakah plant—was committed to the **highest standards of nuclear safety, security, and non-proliferation**.

It also maintained that the program:

- conforms to **IAEA safety standards** and international best practices;
- is subject to international peer reviews, an independent national regulator, and public reporting;
- is intended to be **safe, secure, peaceful, and transparent**; and
- follows non-proliferation commitments, including foregoing domestic uranium enrichment and spent-fuel reprocessing. ([mofa.gov.ae](https://www.mofa.gov.ae/en/MediaHub/News/2019/2019/3/21/21-03-2019-UAE-nuclear-power))

If you mean the **pre-war British Embassy at Wilhelmstraße 70**—the **Palais Strousberg**, used by Britain from the late 1870s until World War II—it looked like a **low, formal 19th-century city palace**, rather than a large modern diplomatic complex.

In photographs it has:

- a long, symmetrical **two-story neoclassical façade**;
- a prominent central porch with **four tall Corinthian columns**;
- a triangular pediment above the columns, with a balustrade running along the roofline;
- evenly spaced, tall rectangular windows and a relatively restrained pale-stone exterior;
- a side carriage entrance/gate, which made it feel like an aristocratic residence adapted for diplomatic use.

The 1896 photograph is particularly clear: despite its grandeur, it was fairly narrow and low compared with the bulky buildings behind it—especially the Hotel Adlon nearby. You can see that historic image in this illustrated worksheet, and a separate archival photograph is available through Google Arts & Culture. ([happy-in-berlin.org](https://happy-in-berlin.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/HiB_Worksheets_BritishEmbassy_DE.pdf))

The building’s interiors were apparently much more lavish: reception rooms, ballroom, library, art gallery, and later a large state hall. The palace was heavily damaged in Allied air raids in 1943 and demolished in 1950. ([en.wikipedia.org](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palais_Strousberg))

If instead you mean the **former British Embassy to East Germany** at **Unter den Linden 32–34**, that was completely different: a restrained, rectangular **1962 East German modernist** building, with a plain, grid-like façade rather than columns and a palace front. A photograph of it is available here. ([commons.wikimedia.org](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABerlin%2C_Mitte%2C_Unter_den_Linden_32-34%2C_Britische_Botschaft_inder_DDR.jpg))
